# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 001
sp("V04-E-001", "Language and media", "Children born into pidgin-speaking communities turn the makeshift pidgin into a full creole language.", "Easy",
   ["When people who share no common language must trade or work together for long periods, they often improvise a pidgin, a stripped-down tongue assembled from pieces of the languages around them.",
    "A pidgin has a small vocabulary, few grammatical endings and a loose word order, and nobody speaks it as a first language.",
    "These children do not simply copy the makeshift speech of their parents.",
    "Within a generation or two they regularise it, adding consistent markers for tense, settled rules of word order and ways of building complex sentences.",
    "The result, called a creole, is a full language in every sense, capable of expressing anything its speakers need.",
    "Linguists have long debated whether this rapid growth reveals something innate in the way children acquire language, or simply reflects the pressures of daily use."],
   "What happens next, when children are born into communities where the pidgin is the main shared language, is one of the more remarkable episodes in the life of languages.",
   [2, 3, 5, 6],
   "The missing sentence introduces children born into pidgin-speaking communities and promises a remarkable development. The next sentence must therefore begin 'These children', which otherwise has no referent, and go on to describe that development. Only gap (1), after the description of the pidgin and before 'These children do not simply copy', allows this.",
   {3: "At gap (2) the paragraph has already begun discussing 'These children' without saying who they are, and announcing 'What happens next' after the children's role has started to be described reverses the order of set-up and development.",
    5: "At gap (3) the creole has just been described as 'a full language in every sense'; announcing what happens next when children are born would come after the outcome it is meant to introduce.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has reached the debate about why the change happens; a sentence setting up the change itself cannot come after the explanation of it, and 'These children' earlier would still lack a referent."},
   "Choosing gap (2) because 'These children' and the missing sentence both mention children, without noticing that 'These children' needs the missing sentence before it.",
   "V04E-pidgins-becoming-creoles", "A dangling 'These children' and a forward-pointing 'What happens next' fix the gap at once.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 002
sp("V04-E-002", "Language and media", "Footnotes exist to make claims checkable, and their abuse calls for better notes rather than none.", "Medium",
   ["Readers outside the academy often treat footnotes as clutter, the visible residue of a scholar's anxiety to seem thorough.",
    "The complaint is understandable, since a page whose lower third is crowded with small print can feel less like an argument than like a legal contract.",
    "Yet the footnote performs a function that the main text cannot.",
    "It tells the reader exactly where a claim came from, so that anyone with patience and a library card can go and check.",
    "A historian who cites a box and folder in such an archive is, in effect, handing her critics the means to test her reading of it.",
    "Admittedly, the device can be abused: a thicket of citations can intimidate rather than inform, and some notes point to sources that do not say what they are claimed to say.",
    "But the remedy for bad footnotes is better ones, not fewer, because prose that cannot be traced to its sources asks to be trusted rather than examined."],
   "That traceability matters most where the evidence is hardest for an ordinary reader to reach, as with the unpublished papers kept in archives.",
   [2, 4, 6, 7],
   "'That traceability' needs a preceding sentence about tracing a claim to its source, which is the sentence ending 'can go and check'. The missing sentence also introduces archives, which the following sentence needs, since it speaks of 'such an archive'. Only gap (2) sits between those two sentences.",
   {2: "At gap (1) nothing about tracing claims has yet been said, so 'That traceability' has no referent; the sentence would also separate the reader's complaint from the 'Yet' that answers it.",
    6: "At gap (3) the sentence would split the concession beginning 'Admittedly' from the 'But' that answers it, and the earlier phrase 'such an archive' would be left with nothing to refer to.",
    7: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already concluded that untraceable prose 'asks to be trusted rather than examined'; bringing in archives after the conclusion is out of place, and 'such an archive' earlier would still dangle."},
   "Choosing gap (4) because 'That traceability' seems to echo 'cannot be traced' in the last sentence, while overlooking that 'such an archive' needs the archive to be introduced earlier.",
   "V04E-footnotes-scholarly-trust", "Requires linking a backward reference to one sentence and a forward reference ('such an archive') to the next; the last sentence offers a tempting verbal echo.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 003
sp("V04-E-003", "Language and media", "Radio drama's lack of pictures was the source of its power, because each listener builds the scene.", "Difficult",
   ["It is usually said that radio drama declined because television could do everything it did and show pictures as well.",
    "The argument assumes that the absence of images was a defect, a gap that early broadcasters filled with sound effects only because they had nothing better.",
    "Those who wrote for the medium understood it differently.",
    "A listener who hears a door creak and footsteps on gravel does not receive a scene; she assembles one, drawing on every door and every path she has known.",
    "The room she imagines is therefore more particular to her than any set designer could make it, and more convincing, because she built it herself.",
    "This is why certain stories, ghost stories above all, can seem more frightening on the radio than on the screen: the monster that is never shown is shaped by each listener's private fears.",
    "None of this means that radio drama was secretly superior, only that its supposed weakness was the very source of its power, and that television replaced it without ever reproducing what it did."],
   "Television, by contrast, builds that room for her, finished down to the wallpaper, and builds the same one for every other viewer.",
   [2, 4, 5, 7],
   "'That room' presupposes 'The room she imagines', and 'builds that room for her' is set 'by contrast' against the listener who 'built it herself'. So the sentence must follow the description of the imagined room. It then supports the next sentence: 'This is why' ghost stories frighten more on radio, because the screen shows the monster while radio leaves it to the listener. Only gap (3) meets both conditions.",
   {2: "At gap (1) no listener and no imagined room have appeared, so 'that room' has nothing to refer to; the sentence would also come between the critics' argument and 'Those who wrote for the medium understood it differently', which answers that argument.",
    4: "At gap (2) the listener has assembled only 'a scene', and no room has yet been mentioned, so 'that room' has no referent; the sentence would also split 'she assembles one' from the following sentence, whose 'therefore' draws directly on it.",
    7: "At gap (4) the final sentence has already named television as the medium that 'replaced it without ever reproducing what it did'; a contrast introduced after that conclusion has no radio-side point beside it to contrast with, and the comparison would arrive after the claim it is meant to support."},
   "Choosing gap (4) because a sentence about television seems to belong next to the closing remark about television, missing that 'that room' and 'for her' tie the contrast to the listener's self-built room.",
   "V04E-radio-drama-imagination", "Two later gaps sit near sentences that also mention television or screens; the solver must see that 'that room' and 'by contrast' tie the sentence to the imagined room and that it grounds the following 'This is why'.",
   exams=HARD)

