sp("V04-E-004", "Language and media", "Euphemisms wear out because attitudes migrate to new words, so renaming can only signal a change that must happen elsewhere.", "Medium",
   ["Words coined to soften an unpleasant subject tend to have short careers.",
    "A term is introduced precisely because the older word has picked up a sting, and for a while the new one sounds neutral, even kind.",
    "But the attitudes that gave the old word its sting have not gone anywhere, and they soon attach themselves to the replacement.",
    "Within a generation the polite term is being used as a taunt in school playgrounds, and reformers begin searching for another.",
    "The cycle can be traced through the vocabulary of illness, disability and poverty, and even through the names for the lavatory, which have turned over again and again.",
    "Some observers conclude from this that the effort to find kinder words is pointless, but that does not quite follow, since a new word can at least signal that the speaker means no harm, even if the signal fades."],
   "The fair conclusion, then, is neither that renaming is futile nor that it works on its own, but that words can only announce a change in feeling that has to happen somewhere else.",
   [1, 3, 5, 6],
   "The missing sentence rejects the view that renaming is 'futile', which answers the observers who call the effort 'pointless', and it accepts the limited value of a signal that 'fades'. It must therefore come after the last sentence, at gap (4), where it settles the debate the paragraph has just opened.",
   {1: "At gap (1) only the short careers of euphemisms have been mentioned; there is no argument yet for 'then' to draw on, and no one has yet suggested that renaming is futile.",
    3: "At gap (2) the cycle is only half described; the polite term has not yet become a taunt, so a conclusion about the whole pattern is premature.",
    5: "At gap (3) the pessimistic view that the effort is 'pointless' has not yet been raised, so a sentence that rejects the claim that renaming is 'futile' would answer an objection before anyone has made it."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because a conclusion seems to belong right after the examples, without noticing that the missing sentence replies to the objection in the final sentence.",
   "V04E-euphemism-treadmill", "The missing sentence reads like a general conclusion that could follow the examples; its reply to 'pointless' is what fixes it at the end.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 005
sp("V04-E-005", "Education", "Memorised number facts free attention for reasoning, provided they are learned after understanding.", "Easy",
   ["Memorising multiplication tables has a poor reputation among educators who want children to understand mathematics rather than recite it.",
    "The worry is reasonable, because a child can chant the tables perfectly and still have no idea when multiplication is the right tool.",
    "Yet memorised facts and understanding are not rivals, for the mind can attend to only a few things at once, and every fact that must be worked out uses up some of that limited space.",
    "A pupil who knows the fact instantly, by contrast, keeps her attention free for the question that matters, which is why the tiles need to be multiplied at all.",
    "Fluency, in other words, is not the enemy of thought but one of its conditions.",
    "None of this justifies drilling tables before children grasp what multiplication means; the case is for memorising facts that are already understood, not for memorising instead of understanding."],
   "A pupil who has to count on her fingers to find seven times eight, in the middle of a problem about the cost of tiles for a floor, will often have lost track of the problem by the time she has the answer.",
   [1, 3, 4, 6],
   "The missing sentence is an example of the claim that working out a fact 'uses up some of that limited space', so it must follow that claim. It must also come before the sentence beginning 'A pupil who knows the fact instantly, by contrast', which contrasts with the slow pupil and refers to 'the tiles' introduced only in the missing sentence. That is gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the paragraph has only stated the poor reputation of memorising; the example would come before both the worry it is meant to answer and the claim about limited attention that it illustrates.",
    4: "At gap (3) the pupil who knows the fact 'by contrast' would already have been described before the pupil she is contrasted with, and 'the tiles' would appear before any tiles had been mentioned.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has moved on to its closing qualification; the example would come too late, after the conclusion it supports, and the earlier 'by contrast' and 'the tiles' would still lack a referent."},
   "Choosing gap (1) because the missing sentence seems to show why educators worry, when in fact it illustrates the cost of not having memorised.",
   "V04E-memorising-multiplication-facts", "An example after its claim, locked by 'by contrast' and 'the tiles' in the next sentence.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 006
sp("V04-E-006", "Education", "Beginners learn more from worked examples than from unaided problem solving, though the advantage reverses as expertise grows.", "Difficult",
   ["A long tradition in teaching holds that people learn to solve problems chiefly by solving them, struggling through difficulties until the method becomes their own.",
    "For learners who already know a good deal, that tradition is largely sound.",
    "Such learners often reach an answer by trial and error and come away with no clearer idea of the method than they had before.",
    "Studying a fully worked solution, step by step, spares them the search and directs their attention to the structure of the solution itself.",
    "The advantage does not last, however: as competence grows, the same worked examples become redundant, and learners who are made to study them gain less than those left to tackle problems on their own.",
    "The sensible conclusion is not that one method is right, but that the right method depends on where the learner stands."],
   "For genuine beginners, however, unaided struggle tends to be a poor teacher, because it consumes their attention in searching for a route rather than in noticing why the route works.",
   [2, 3, 5, 6],
   "Without the missing sentence, 'Such learners', who flounder by trial and error, would refer to those 'who already know a good deal', which makes no sense. The missing sentence supplies the contrast with experienced learners ('however') and introduces the beginners that 'Such learners' must mean. It therefore belongs at gap (1), between the remark about experienced learners and the sentence beginning 'Such learners'.",
   {3: "At gap (2) the sentence about trial and error would already have been attributed to experienced learners, which contradicts the claim that the tradition suits them; the 'however' would then contrast beginners with a sentence that already describes beginners.",
    5: "At gap (3) the paragraph is turning, through 'The advantage does not last, however', to the limits of worked examples for more competent learners; reintroducing beginners and their search here would interrupt that turn and repeat what has been shown.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already drawn its conclusion that 'the right method depends on where the learner stands'; a sentence that first introduces beginners cannot follow the conclusion that depends on them."},
   "Leaving 'Such learners' attached to experienced learners because it sits right after them, and so choosing a later gap.",
   "V04E-worked-examples-novices", "The text without the sentence looks superficially continuous; the solver must notice that 'Such learners' would refer to the wrong group.",
   exams=HARD)

