sp("V04-E-007", "Education", "Learning to read first in the home language avoids a double burden and leads more surely to the dominant language.", "Medium",
   ["Across much of the multilingual world, pupils start their schooling in a language seldom spoken in their homes, typically because it is the language of the state, the universities and the better-paid professions.",
    "The intention is to give them an early start in the language that will matter most for their futures.",
    "In practice, a child who must learn to read in an unfamiliar tongue faces two tasks at once: decoding print and making sense of words she does not yet know.",
    "The result is that many such children fall behind in the early years in a way that later teaching struggles to undo.",
    "Children who first learn to read in their home language face only the first task, and once they can read, the skill carries over to a second language with surprising ease.",
    "This is why the case for teaching in the mother tongue during the first years is not a case against the dominant language but a better route towards it.",
    "Parents who want their children taught in the language of opportunity from the first day are not wrong about where opportunity lies; they are mistaken only about the quickest way to reach it."],
   "Either task alone would tax a six-year-old, and the two together compete for the same limited attention.",
   [1, 2, 3, 6],
   "'Either task' and 'the two together' refer back to the 'two tasks at once' of decoding print and understanding unfamiliar words. The sentence also explains 'The result', that many such children fall behind. It must therefore sit between those two sentences, at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) only the choice of school language has been mentioned; no tasks have been named, so 'Either task' has no referent.",
    2: "At gap (2) the paragraph has stated the intention behind the policy but has not yet described the two tasks, so 'Either task' still has nothing to refer to.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already argued for mother-tongue teaching as 'a better route'; returning to the double burden here would break the move from that conclusion to the remark about parents."},
   "Choosing gap (2) on the feeling that difficulty should be mentioned as soon as the policy is described, before the tasks themselves are named.",
   "V04E-mother-tongue-early-schooling", "The backward reference 'Either task' must be matched to the 'two tasks' sentence, and the explanation must precede 'The result'.")

