sp("V04-E-008", "Education", "Smaller classes are an opportunity for better teaching, not a guarantee of it.", "Medium",
   ["Few school reforms are as popular with parents and teachers as smaller classes.",
    "The appeal is intuitive: with fewer pupils, a teacher should be able to notice each child's difficulties and respond to them.",
    "The evidence, however, is more mixed than the enthusiasm suggests.",
    "Modest cuts, from thirty-two pupils to twenty-eight, say, seem to change little, perhaps because teachers carry on teaching in exactly the way they did before.",
    "Larger cuts can help, particularly for the youngest children, but they are expensive and require many new teachers, some of whom will be less experienced than those already in post.",
    "The same money might in some cases do more good if it were spent on improving the teaching that pupils already receive."],
   "Smaller classes, in short, create an opportunity for better teaching rather than guaranteeing it, and if the opportunity goes unused, that money has bought very little.",
   [1, 2, 4, 6],
   "'In short' signals a summary of the whole argument: small cuts change little when teaching stays the same, larger cuts cost a lot, and the money could go to teaching itself. Its phrase 'that money' needs money to have been mentioned, and it is first mentioned only in the final sentence, 'The same money', after the cost of larger cuts. So the sentence closes the paragraph at gap (4).",
   {1: "At gap (1) only the popularity of smaller classes has been stated; there is nothing yet to sum up, and the sentence would come between that popularity and 'The appeal', which explains it.",
    2: "At gap (2) the paragraph has given only the intuitive case for smaller classes; a sceptical summary here would come before the evidence, and the 'however' in the next sentence would then contrast with a conclusion that already agrees with it.",
    4: "At gap (3) the sentence would split the contrast between 'Modest cuts' and 'Larger cuts', and no money has yet been mentioned, so 'that money' would have nothing to refer to; 'in short' would also sum up an argument that is only half made."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because the remark that teachers carry on as before seems to prompt the point about unused opportunity, without seeing that the cost argument comes later.",
   "V04E-class-size-reduction", "A summarising conclusion; gap (3) is tempting because teachers carrying on as before looks like an unused opportunity, and 'that money' is what chiefly rules it out.")

