sp("V04-E-010", "Health and medicine", "Emergency triage seems unfair to those who wait but rests on a different idea of fairness, which demands good communication.", "Medium",
   ["Emergency departments do not treat patients in the order in which they arrive.",
    "Instead, a nurse makes a rapid assessment of each newcomer and assigns a priority based on how quickly the patient's condition is likely to worsen without help.",
    "The principle can seem unfair to those who wait, since it rewards neither patience nor punctuality.",
    "Yet the alternative, a queue in strict order of arrival, would mean that a patient whose life depended on speed might die behind a dozen who could safely have waited.",
    "Triage, in other words, does not ignore fairness but rests on a different idea of it, one that weighs the cost of waiting rather than the time already spent.",
    "Even so, a department that applies the principle well can still fail its patients by leaving them uninformed, and it is often the silence, rather than the order of treatment, that waiting patients resent."],
   "A man with a broken wrist who has waited patiently since early evening may watch a woman who arrived an hour ago with chest pain go straight through to a doctor.",
   [3, 4, 5, 6],
   "The missing sentence is a concrete case of the claim that triage 'rewards neither patience nor punctuality': the patient who waited longer is passed over. It must follow that claim and come before 'Yet the alternative', which answers the complaint. That is gap (1).",
   {4: "At gap (2) the paragraph has already replied with 'Yet the alternative'; an example of the complaint would now interrupt the move from that reply to its restatement with 'in other words'.",
    5: "At gap (3) the defence of triage as 'a different idea' of fairness is complete; an example of the original complaint here would reopen what has just been settled.",
    6: "At gap (4) the last sentence concerns the silence that patients resent rather than the order of treatment; an example about who is seen first would be about the issue the sentence has just set aside."},
   "Choosing gap (4) because the example features a waiting patient and the last sentence mentions waiting patients, missing that it illustrates the order of treatment, not communication.",
   "V04E-emergency-triage", "Example after its claim; the later gaps tempt because they also mention waiting.")

