sp("V04-E-011", "Health and medicine", "Surgical checklists work by changing how the team talks, not by supplying knowledge, so box-ticking versions fail.", "Difficult",
   ["When hospitals began asking surgical teams to work through a short checklist before each operation, many surgeons were insulted.",
    "They did not need, they said, a printed card to remind them to confirm the patient's identity or check which side of the body was to be operated on.",
    "In one sense they were right: almost nothing on such lists is unknown to a qualified surgeon.",
    "A pause in which every member of the team introduces themselves and states a concern makes it easier for a junior nurse to point out, minutes later, that something looks wrong.",
    "In a setting where authority runs steeply from the surgeon downwards, that permission to speak can matter more than any single item on the card.",
    "It also explains a less welcome observation: where checklists are introduced as paperwork, ticked by one person in a corner, they tend to achieve very little.",
    "The card is not the cure; the conversation it forces is."],
   "But the lists were never meant to supply knowledge; their value lay in what the ritual of working through them aloud did to the people in the room.",
   [2, 3, 5, 7],
   "'In one sense they were right' concedes that surgeons already know what is on the list; the missing sentence answers that concession with 'But' and turns from knowledge to 'the ritual' and 'the people in the room'. The next sentence describes exactly that ritual, the pause in which the team speaks. Only gap (2) puts the sentence after the concession and before the description of the pause.",
   {2: "At gap (1) the rebuttal would come before the concession it answers; 'In one sense they were right' would then follow a 'But' that has already moved on, and the paragraph would pass from that concession to the team pause with no turn at all.",
    5: "At gap (3) the effect of the ritual on the people in the room has already been shown through the junior nurse and the 'permission to speak'; announcing that value here repeats it, and it separates that point from 'It also explains', which builds on it.",
    7: "At gap (4) the paragraph has already ended on its summary, 'the conversation it forces'; a 'But' after that has nothing to oppose, and the point would arrive after everything it was meant to introduce."},
   "Choosing gap (1) because 'But' seems to answer the surgeons' complaint directly, overlooking that the concession 'In one sense they were right' must come before the rebuttal.",
   "V04E-surgical-checklists", "Two early gaps both follow the surgeons' objection; telling them apart requires seeing the concession and reply structure.",
   exams=HARD)

