sp("V04-E-012", "Health and medicine", "Adding iodine to salt is a model of cheap, quiet, population-wide prevention.", "Easy",
   ["Iodine deficiency was once widespread in upland districts and in regions far from the sea, where the soil holds little of the element.",
    "Its most visible sign was goitre, a swelling of the thyroid gland in the neck, but its gravest effect was hidden: children born to deficient mothers could suffer lasting harm to their mental development.",
    "The remedy turned out to be remarkably simple.",
    "Since salt is eaten by almost everybody, in quantities that vary little from day to day, a tiny addition of iodine reaches nearly the whole population with no change of habit required.",
    "The cost is small, the taste is unaffected, and no one needs to remember to take a pill.",
    "Where such programmes have faltered, it has usually been because of lapses in production or monitoring rather than any flaw in the idea itself."],
   "Iodised salt thus stands as a model of public health at its best: a serious harm prevented quietly, cheaply and on a vast scale.",
   [1, 2, 3, 6],
   "The missing sentence is a conclusion ('thus') that draws on the harm, the simplicity, the low cost and the reach of the remedy, all of which come before it. It must therefore close the paragraph, at gap (4), after the remark that failures came from lapses rather than from the idea.",
   {1: "At gap (1) only the existence of the deficiency has been mentioned; no remedy has been described, so 'thus' has nothing to draw on.",
    2: "At gap (2) the harm has been described but not the remedy, so the sentence would praise iodised salt before it has been introduced.",
    3: "At gap (3) the paragraph has just promised that the remedy was 'remarkably simple', which must be followed directly by the explanation of how it works, not by the verdict."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because 'remarkably simple' sounds like the praise in the missing sentence, without noticing that the explanation must come first.",
   "V04E-iodised-salt", "A clear concluding sentence with 'thus'; all wrong gaps come before the remedy is explained.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

