sp("V04-E-013", "Cities and geography", "Desire paths worn across parks are evidence of how places are really used and deserve attention from designers.", "Easy",
   ["Walk across almost any park and you will find them: narrow strips of bare earth cutting diagonally across the grass, worn by people who preferred a shorter route to the one the designers laid out.",
    "Some designers now wait a season or two after a park opens, watch where people actually walk, and only then pave the routes that feet have already chosen.",
    "The result is a set of paths that people use, rather than ones they ignore.",
    "The approach has its limits, since the shortest route is not always the safest, and a track worn across a fragile meadow may destroy the very thing the park exists to protect.",
    "Still, the worn strip of earth is a kind of evidence, recorded without anyone filling in a survey, about how a place is really used.",
    "A designer who ignores it is choosing to trust a drawing over the people for whom the drawing was made."],
   "Planners have traditionally treated these desire paths as a nuisance to be fenced off or replanted, but a different attitude is possible.",
   [1, 2, 4, 6],
   "'These desire paths' refers back to the worn strips described in the first sentence, and 'a different attitude is possible' sets up the next sentence, which describes designers who wait and then pave the chosen routes. The sentence therefore belongs at gap (1).",
   {2: "At gap (2) the different attitude has already been described in the sentence about designers who 'pave the routes that feet have already chosen', so announcing that one is 'possible' comes too late.",
    4: "At gap (3) the paragraph is weighing the limits of the new approach; 'these desire paths' would be far from the strips it refers to, and the alternative attitude has long since been introduced.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has reached its final judgement on designers who ignore worn paths; a sentence that only sets up the alternative cannot come after the conclusion."},
   "Choosing gap (2) because the traditional attitude seems to contrast with the new one, without noticing that the new one has already been described.",
   "V04E-desire-paths-parks", "A forward set-up sentence with a clear backward reference; the alternatives come after what it introduces.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 014
sp("V04-E-014", "Cities and geography", "Widening roads often fails because traffic responds to the time cost of travel rather than being a fixed quantity.", "Difficult",
   ["The intuitive response to a congested road is to widen it.",
    "For a while the extra lanes do their job, and journeys that once crawled begin to flow.",
    "But the faster journey is itself an invitation.",
    "People who had avoided the road at peak hours begin to use it, some who had taken the train return to their cars, and new houses are built further out along the route because commuting from them has become bearable.",
    "Within a few years the road is often as crowded as it was before, now carrying more vehicles at much the same crawl.",
    "Neither observation shows that roads should never be built; together they show that traffic is not a fixed quantity to be accommodated, like water to be piped, but a response to the price of travelling, measured largely in time.",
    "Congestion is that price rising, and adding capacity lowers it only until demand has caught up."],
   "The reverse has sometimes been observed as well: when a major road is closed, the expected gridlock on neighbouring streets can fail to appear, as some trips are rerouted, retimed or simply abandoned.",
   [2, 4, 5, 6],
   "'The reverse' needs the whole story of widening, ending with the road 'as crowded as it was before', to be complete. The following sentence, 'Neither observation', needs two observations, and the road closure is the second. The sentence therefore sits between the end of the widening story and 'Neither observation', at gap (3).",
   {2: "At gap (1) the extra lanes have only just begun to work; the 'invitation' and the return of congestion have not been described, so there is no complete pattern yet for a road closure to reverse.",
    4: "At gap (2) the sentence would interrupt the widening story before its outcome; the next sentence, 'Within a few years the road is often as crowded', would then seem to describe the closed road, which makes no sense.",
    6: "At gap (4) 'Neither observation' would already have been used before the second observation appeared, and the sentence would separate 'that price' in the last sentence from the 'price of travelling' it refers to."},
   "Choosing gap (2) because the list of new users seems to be a natural place to mention journeys that are abandoned, without seeing that the widening story must finish first.",
   "V04E-induced-traffic-demand", "The solver must see that 'Neither observation' counts two observations, one of which is only in the missing sentence, while 'The reverse' requires the first to be complete.",
   exams=HARD)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 015
sp("V04-E-015", "Cities and geography", "Mountain ranges create rain shadows, so a ridge can matter more to rainfall than latitude or distance.", "Medium",
   ["Travel inland across certain mountain ranges and the landscape can change within a few hours' drive from dripping forest to dry scrub.",
    "The cause lies in what happens to moist air when it meets the mountains.",
    "Forced upwards by the slope, the air cools, and because cool air can hold less water vapour than warm air, much of its moisture condenses and falls as rain or snow on the windward side.",
    "The lee slopes and the plains beyond, swept by this dry, sinking air, therefore lie in what geographers call a rain shadow, receiving a fraction of the rainfall that soaks the other side.",
    "Some of the world's driest regions owe their aridity less to their distance from the sea than to a range of mountains standing between them and the prevailing winds.",
    "The point is worth remembering whenever a map is read too simply, as though rainfall were a matter of latitude alone: a single ridge can matter more than hundreds of kilometres."],
   "By the time the air has crossed the crest and begins to descend on the far side, it has shed much of its water, and it grows warmer and drier as it sinks.",
   [2, 3, 4, 6],
   "The sentence describes the next stage of the air's journey: after rising and losing moisture on the windward side, it crosses the crest and descends. It must follow the account of the rising air and come before the sentence about the lee slopes 'swept by this dry, sinking air', which needs the descent to have been described. That is gap (2).",
   {2: "At gap (1) the air has not yet been forced upwards or lost its moisture; having it cross the crest and descend before it has climbed reverses the sequence.",
    4: "At gap (3) the rain shadow has already been named as the result, and 'this dry, sinking air' in that sentence would have appeared before any sinking air was described; the step in the process would arrive after its own consequence.",
    6: "At gap (4) the paragraph has moved from the mechanism to its lesson for reading maps; returning to the descending air at the end is out of place."},
   "Choosing gap (3) on the view that the descent explains the dry regions mentioned next, overlooking that 'this dry, sinking air' in the rain-shadow sentence already depends on it.",
   "V04E-rain-shadow-deserts", "A step in a causal sequence; the solver must place it between the ascent and the consequence, which refers back to 'this dry, sinking air'.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 016
sp("V04-E-016", "Cities and geography", "The street grid, though often dismissed as dull, is legible, offers many routes and allows gradual rebuilding.", "Medium",
   ["Grids of right-angled streets are often written off as unimaginative, the work of surveyors who cared more about parcelling out land quickly than about making beautiful places.",
    "There is some truth in the charge, since a grid laid over hills and rivers without regard for them can produce streets that are absurdly steep or that end abruptly at the water.",
    "Yet the grid has virtues that its critics tend to overlook.",
    "It is easy to find one's way in, because a newcomer who knows two street names can work out how to travel between them.",
    "It offers many alternative routes, so that traffic and pedestrians are not all funnelled onto a few main roads.",
    "And because its blocks are regular, they can be divided, combined and rebuilt over centuries as uses change, without the whole plan having to be redrawn."],
   "The winding lanes of an old quarter, by contrast, may delight the visitor but tend to resist exactly this kind of gradual reinvention.",
   [2, 3, 5, 6],
   "'This kind of gradual reinvention' refers to blocks being 'divided, combined and rebuilt over centuries', which is described only in the last sentence. 'By contrast' sets the old quarter against that virtue of the grid. The sentence must therefore follow the last sentence, at gap (4).",
   {2: "At gap (1) the paragraph has only stated the charge against the grid; no reinvention has been mentioned, so 'this kind of gradual reinvention' has no referent.",
    3: "At gap (2) the virtues have been promised but not yet listed, so there is nothing for the old quarter to be contrasted with.",
    5: "At gap (3) the preceding sentence is about alternative routes, not reinvention, and the sentence would interrupt the list of virtues before 'And because', which completes it."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because winding lanes seem to contrast with the many routes of the grid, without noticing that 'reinvention' refers to the rebuilding of blocks.",
   "V04E-street-grid-layouts", "The contrast marker must be matched with the specific virtue it opposes; one wrong gap offers a plausible contrast about routes.")

