sp("V04-E-017", "Cities and geography", "Containerisation emptied city waterfronts, which were opened to the public only once they stopped being workplaces.", "Difficult",
   ["For most of their history, port cities were organised around the water's edge, where ships were loaded and unloaded by large crews of dock workers.",
    "The docks, the warehouses and the streets around them formed a dense, noisy working district, closed to casual visitors but central to the city's life.",
    "The shipping container changed that arrangement within a few decades.",
    "The new terminals were therefore built downstream or on open land at the edge of the city, and the old docks fell silent.",
    "When the abandoned waterfronts were later redeveloped as promenades, museums and apartments, the change was celebrated as a return of the water to the public.",
    "There is something to that description, since few residents had ever been able to walk along the old quays.",
    "But it is a strange kind of return, in which the waterfront becomes open to everyone at precisely the moment it stops being a place where anyone works."],
   "Ships built to carry thousands of identical steel boxes needed deep berths and acres of flat ground for stacking, and the cramped quays at the heart of the old city could offer neither.",
   [3, 4, 5, 6],
   "The missing sentence explains how the container changed things: the new ships needed space that the old quays lacked. That is the reason the next sentence draws on with 'therefore' when it says new terminals were built at the edge of the city. The sentence must come between the remark that the container 'changed that arrangement' and the 'therefore' sentence, at gap (1).",
   {4: "At gap (2) the sentence would give the reason only after its consequence had been stated, leaving 'therefore' in the sentence about new terminals with nothing before it to draw on.",
    5: "At gap (3) the paragraph has moved on to the redevelopment of the abandoned waterfronts; a technical point about berths and stacking space would interrupt that later story.",
    6: "At gap (4) the sentence would separate the concession 'There is something to that description' from the 'But' that qualifies it, and it has nothing to do with the claim that the waterfront was returned to the public."},
   "Choosing gap (2) because the needs of container ships seem to explain why the old docks fell silent, overlooking that 'therefore' already requires that reason earlier.",
   "V04E-container-ports-waterfronts", "Gap (2) offers a plausible after-the-fact explanation; rejecting it depends on noticing the 'therefore' that needs the reason before it.",
   exams=HARD)

