sp("V04-E-018", "Philosophy and ethics", "The ship rebuilt from its old planks shows that our idea of sameness bundles criteria that can come apart.", "Medium",
   ["Imagine a wooden ship whose planks are replaced one at a time as they rot, until after many years not a single original plank remains.",
    "Most people asked about such a case say that it is still the same ship, because the change was gradual and the ship never ceased to sail.",
    "That answer appeals to continuity: what makes something the same thing over time is an unbroken history, not the persistence of its material.",
    "Suppose that someone has quietly collected every discarded plank and, at the end, reassembles them in their original arrangement.",
    "Now there are two ships, and each has a claim: one has the unbroken history, the other has all the original material.",
    "Whichever we choose, we seem to give up something our first answer relied upon, which suggests that our ordinary idea of sameness bundles together criteria that usually agree but can come apart."],
   "The puzzle becomes sharper, however, if we add one further detail to the story.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence announces a 'further detail' and so must come just before that detail, which is the collecting and reassembling of the old planks. 'However' marks a turn away from the comfortable answer about continuity. Both conditions place it at gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the next sentence does not add any detail; it returns to the original case with 'such a case', so the promised detail would never arrive where it was announced.",
    2: "At gap (2) the sentence would come between the answer that the ship is the same and 'That answer', which must follow it directly.",
    5: "At gap (4) the further detail has already been added and its consequences drawn; announcing a detail that is then never given would leave the sentence pointing at nothing."},
   "Choosing gap (1) because the puzzle seems to sharpen as soon as the case is described, without checking that a new detail must follow the sentence.",
   "V04E-ship-of-theseus-identity", "A forward set-up sentence; the solver must check which gap is followed by the new detail it promises.")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- 019
sp("V04-E-019", "Philosophy and ethics", "Promises bind even when no one relies on them, so promissory obligation cannot rest only on created expectations.", "Difficult",
   ["A promise is a curious thing: by uttering a few words, I bind myself to a duty that, a moment earlier, was nowhere to be found.",
    "One popular explanation is that promising creates expectations in others, who then rely on me and stand to be harmed if I let them down.",
    "Suppose I promise a dying friend to look after her garden, and she dies within the hour, before anyone else has learned of my promise.",
    "No one now expects anything of me, and no one will be disappointed if the garden runs wild.",
    "Yet most people feel that I would still be doing something wrong in breaking my word.",
    "If they are right, the obligation must rest on something beyond the harm of dashed expectations, perhaps on the trust that was placed in me at the moment I spoke, which does not lapse merely because the one who gave it can no longer check."],
   "The trouble is that this account cannot easily explain promises on which nobody relies at all, and such promises are not hard to imagine.",
   [1, 2, 5, 6],
   "'This account' must refer to the expectation explanation, so the sentence must follow it, and 'such promises are not hard to imagine' announces the case of the dying friend that the next sentence asks us to suppose. The sentence therefore introduces the counterexample at gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) no account of promising has yet been given; the first sentence only states the puzzle, so 'this account' has nothing to refer to.",
    5: "At gap (3) such a promise has already been imagined, so saying that one is 'not hard to imagine' comes too late; the sentence would also separate 'most people feel' from 'If they are right', so that 'they' would seem to refer to the promises rather than to the people who judge them.",
    6: "At gap (4) the last sentence has just proposed the trust account; 'this account' would now seem to point to that proposal, which the paragraph favours, and the difficulty would be raised after it has already been answered."},
   "Choosing gap (3) because the difficulty seems to be shown by the reaction of 'most people', without seeing that it breaks the link between 'most people' and 'they'.",
   "V04E-promises-obligation", "Two later gaps follow parts of the counterexample; rejecting them requires tracking 'this account' and the pronoun 'they'.",
   exams=HARD)

