sp("V04-E-020", "Philosophy and ethics", "White lies do harm by cumulatively draining praise of its meaning, though honesty need not be cruel.", "Medium",
   ["White lies are usually defended as small kindnesses that smooth social life at no real cost to anyone.",
    "Telling a friend that a dull dinner was delightful spares her feelings, and it is hard to see whom the lie harms.",
    "The harm, if there is one, is not to be found in any single case.",
    "It accumulates, because each lie of this kind slightly lowers the value of the words used to tell it.",
    "Someone known to praise everything soon finds that her praise carries no information.",
    "None of this requires us to become blunt or cruel; there is usually something both true and kind to be said, if we take the trouble to look for it before reaching for the easy falsehood."],
   "When she tells a nervous colleague that his speech was genuinely moving, he has no way of knowing whether she means it, and the compliment he most needed is worth nothing to him.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence is an example of the claim that praise from 'Someone known to praise everything' carries 'no information': 'she' is that person, and her sincere compliment cannot be trusted. It must follow that claim and come before the closing qualification, at gap (4).",
   {1: "At gap (1) no one has yet been described whose praise is discounted, so 'she' has no referent, and the example would show a harm before the paragraph has even stated the defence of white lies.",
    2: "At gap (2) the paragraph has just said 'it is hard to see whom the lie harms'; giving the harm here would come before 'The harm, if there is one', which treats the harm as still to be found.",
    3: "At gap (3) the sentence would separate 'The harm, if there is one' from 'It accumulates', which must follow it, and the mechanism by which praise loses value has not yet been stated."},
   "Choosing gap (2) because the dinner host seems to be the 'she' of the missing sentence, without noticing that the example illustrates the cumulative harm stated later.",
   "V04E-white-lies-trust", "An example after its claim; the pronoun 'she' has a misleading candidate in the dinner sentence.")

