sp("V04-E-022", "Psychology", "A wide range of options can deter choosing and reduce satisfaction, though the effect is limited.", "Easy",
   ["It seems obvious that more choice is better: if a shop offers forty kinds of jam instead of six, every customer who would have been happy before can still be happy, and some will find exactly what they wanted.",
    "Yet a very wide range of options can sometimes leave people less willing to choose at all, and less satisfied with what they eventually pick.",
    "Even those who do buy are more likely to wonder whether one of the thirty-nine they rejected would have been better.",
    "The effect is not universal, and it seems weakest when people already know what they want or when the options are clearly arranged.",
    "But it is a useful reminder that choice has costs as well as benefits, paid in time, in effort and in the nagging sense of a better option missed."],
   "A shopper faced with forty jams may compare them for several minutes, grow weary of weighing small differences, and walk away with none.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence illustrates the claim that many options can leave people 'less willing to choose at all': the shopper walks away with none. It must follow that claim and come before 'Even those who do buy', which contrasts with the shopper who does not. That is gap (2).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the paragraph has only stated that more choice seems better; an example of a shopper giving up would come before the claim it illustrates, and the 'Yet' of the next sentence would oppose an example that supports it.",
    3: "At gap (3) 'Even those who do buy' would already have appeared without any non-buyer before it, and the example would come after the paragraph has moved on to dissatisfaction.",
    5: "At gap (4) the paragraph has qualified the effect and drawn its lesson; an example of the effect after the conclusion would be out of place."},
   "Choosing gap (1) because the example uses the same forty jams as the opening sentence, without noticing that it illustrates the claim made in the next one.",
   "V04E-choice-overload", "An example after its claim, locked by 'Even those who do buy'.",
   cognitive="Understanding")

