sp("V04-E-023", "Psychology", "Ignoring sunk costs is sound advice, but past investment can carry information and value, so the error is letting it replace fresh judgement.", "Medium",
   ["Economists advise that money already spent and unrecoverable should play no part in deciding what to do next.",
    "If a concert ticket cannot be refunded, the only question on a stormy evening is whether going will be more enjoyable than staying at home; the price paid is gone either way.",
    "People who follow this advice avoid a familiar trap, that of pouring further resources into a failing project simply because so much has already been invested.",
    "Past investment sometimes carries information: a firm that has spent heavily on a project may know things about its prospects that an outsider does not.",
    "And for individuals, honouring a past commitment can be a way of remaining the kind of person who finishes things, a reputation with value of its own.",
    "The error lies not in remembering what has been spent, but in letting the memory substitute for a fresh judgement about what lies ahead."],
   "Yet the rule, sound as it is, can be applied too mechanically.",
   [1, 2, 3, 5],
   "The missing sentence turns from praise of the rule to its limits. Everything before gap (3) supports the rule: the advice, the ticket example and the trap it avoids. Everything after it qualifies the rule: past investment can carry information, and commitments can have value. 'Yet' therefore belongs at the turning point, gap (3).",
   {1: "At gap (1) the rule has only just been stated; 'sound as it is' has not yet been shown, and the 'Yet' would be followed by the ticket example, which shows the rule working rather than being misapplied.",
    2: "At gap (2) the turn would be followed by more praise of the rule, the trap it helps people avoid, so the promised limits would not arrive.",
    5: "At gap (4) both limits have already been given; announcing that the rule can be applied too mechanically after showing how comes in the wrong order and would split the second limit from the conclusion that sums them up."},
   "Choosing gap (2) because the ticket example seems to show the rule at its most mechanical, without noticing that the next sentence still praises the rule.",
   "V04E-sunk-cost-fallacy", "A contrast marker that must be placed exactly at the turn from support to qualification.")

